<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anna of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1693">1693</a>–<a href="/years/1740">1740</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Anna Ioannovna · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">House of Romanov</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anna Ioannovna was Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from 1730 to 1740, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>. Before her accession she was duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, having married its duke, who died in 1711. As empress she tore up the conditions the nobles had tried to impose on her and relied on German advisers, above all her favourite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-johann-von-biron">Ernst Johann von Biron</a>, whom she made duke of Courland in 1737.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anna-of-russia.jpg" alt="Anna of Russia" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Caravaque · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Caravaque,_Portrait_of_Empress_Anna_Ioannovna_(1730).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1731 Abulkhair Khan of the Junior Juz swore allegiance to Empress <b>Anna of Russia</b>, and parts of the Middle Juz followed; the fortress of Orenburg was founded in 1743 as a base on the steppe frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century Russian influence grew; Ernst Johann von Biron, favourite of <b>Empress Anna</b>, became duke in 1737 and built Rundāle Palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1723" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anna of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1723">What was here in 1723? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
